{"created": "2026-05-29T13:02:22.660809+00:00", "modified": "2026-05-29T13:02:22.876493+00:00", "id": 20445734, "conceptrecid": "20445733", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.20445733", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "metadata": {"title": "Fano Duality Projective Orthogonality and The CSS Bridge", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "publication_date": "2026-05-29", "description": "<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">Paper 7 of this series constructed an explicit Clifford representation &rho;: ZD*(4) &rarr; Cliff(0,7) and observed, via exhaustive computation, that the triple-product identity &rho;\u0303(e_p)&rho;\u0303(e_r)&rho;\u0303(e_t) = &phi; &middot; Q_{p,r,t} defines a bijection from the 7 Fano lines to the 7 non-zero elements of {I,Z}^&otimes;3, coinciding with the projective duality of PG(2,\ud835\udd3d\u2082). That paper left open whether this duality is the X/Z duality of the Steane [[7,1,3]] code.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">This paper closes the gap and proves the following.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\"><strong>Main Theorem (CSS Bridge, n=4).</strong> For every Fano line {p,r,t}, the Z-string Q_{p,r,t} equals the projective orthogonal complement of {p,r,t} in \ud835\udd3d\u2082&sup3;, and coincides with the unique Z-stabiliser of the Steane [[7,1,3]] code whose support is the complement {1,...,7} \\ {p,r,t}. The correspondence is equivariant for the contragredient action of GL(3,\ud835\udd3d\u2082): lines transform covariantly, their dual Z-stabilisers transform contravariantly via g^{-T}.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\"><strong>Theorem (n=5 CSS Bridge).</strong> The analogue holds for n=5: every hyperplane W of PG(3,\ud835\udd3d\u2082) maps to the unique Z-stabiliser of the [[15,7,3]] Reed&ndash;Muller code with support equal to the complement of W. Here M = I\u2084 (no bit-reversal needed).</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\"><strong>Proposition (M = I_{n-1} for all n &ge; 2).</strong> Under the MSB-first generator convention, the CSS bridge S_Z(v_W) = {1,...,2^{n-1}-1} \\ W holds analytically for all n &ge; 2, with M = I_{n-1}.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">As a corollary, the sign-parity constraint &pi;_A &pi;_B &pi;_C = &minus;1 (proved in the series anchor paper) acquires a unified three-way interpretation: algebraically as a sign-parity discriminant, quantum-error-correcting as the phase of a balanced ZD triple, and projective-geometrically as the orthogonality relation between a Fano line and its dual point in PG(2,\ud835\udd3d\u2082).</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">A second corollary makes precise why no Clifford representation can lift the full octonion multiplication to a group algebra: the 28 non-Fano triples do not satisfy the cocycle condition, and no Z-type projective dual can be assigned to them.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">Part of a series on zero-divisors in Cayley&ndash;Dickson algebras. Series anchor: <a class=\"underline underline underline-offset-2 decoration-1 decoration-current/40 hover:decoration-current focus:decoration-current\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20315751\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20315751</a></p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Levratti, Giovanni", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0000-9804-3452"}], "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "20445733"}}]}}, "title": "Fano Duality Projective Orthogonality and The CSS Bridge", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20445734", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/20445734", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/20445734?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.20445734", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20445734/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20445733", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/20445733", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20445733", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.20445733", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20445734/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20445734/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "50": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E50,/0/default.jpg", "100": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E100,/0/default.jpg", "250": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E250,/0/default.jpg", "750": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E750,/0/default.jpg", "1200": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20445734:FANO%20DUALITY%20PROJECTIVE%20ORTHOGONALITY%20AND%20THE%20CSS%20BRIDGE_v1.pdf/full/%5E1200,/0/default.jpg"}, "latest": "https://zenodo.org/api/r